    'nuances' => array(
        'SUPERSEDES the earlier instruction that „powered by expertise” must be „ne alimentează expertiza”. With an abstract subject such as expertise, knowledge or experience, „a alimenta” (to feed/fuel) is a mechanical transfer of the English metaphor: write „Ne ghidează un scop și ne susține expertiza.” Keep „a alimenta” only for literal fuel, power and supply lines.',
        'Lock the Romanian form of “cookie” across the whole consent flow: the plural is „cookie-uri” (definite „cookie-urile”) or „module cookie”, never the bare English plural „cookies”. Use the SAME form in the banner, the rejection toast, the preferences panel and the footer link — „Folosim cookie-uri și tehnologii similare…” beside „Doar cookie-urile esențiale sunt active.” Romanian loanwords take the enclitic article and a Romanian plural.',
        'A standalone “More” that heads a group of additional links or cards is the plural adjective „Mai multe”, not the adverb „Mai mult” (which means more in degree or quantity). Keep „Mai mult” only after a verb („Citiți mai mult”). Check every bare More / All / Other menu label for this before delivering.',
        'Do not repeat in the value a noun that already heads the label, even when English does: “Priority 1: High Priority” = „Prioritate 1: înaltă”, „Prioritate 2: medie”, „Prioritate 3: scăzută / de rutină”. Romanian reads the repeated head noun as clumsy filler. The same applies to “Type: Request type”, “Status: Open status” and similar label:value pairs.',
        'Form field labels may not be bare noun+noun stacks even when the English is: “Contact name” = „Nume de contact” (or „Persoană de contact”), never „Nume contact”; “Company name” = „Denumirea companiei”. Insert „de” or a genitive — a two-noun juxtaposition reads as telegraphic machine output in a Romanian form.',
        'REFINES the rule about inserting a separator between an untranslated brand name and a translated descriptor: mirror EXACTLY the punctuation of the English sibling label. If the English reads „Ascential Care - Available service level agreements” keep the hyphen; if it reads „Ascential Care Support Services” with no hyphen, the Romanian carries no hyphen either („Ascential Care Servicii de asistență”). Never add a separator the English item lacks just because a neighbouring item has one.',
        'Consent, cookie and privacy WIDGET configuration strings are visitor-facing copy in a GDPR jurisdiction and must be translated with the same care as body copy — table headers („Nume”, „Domeniu”, „Durată”), duration values („2 ani”, „1 an”, „1 zi”, „3 luni”, „30 de minute”, „10 minute”, „sesiune”, note the mandatory „de” after 30), plural count words („cookie” / „cookie-uri”), the policy link label („Politica de confidențialitate”) and any data-transfer disclosure sentence. Translating the cookie descriptions but leaving the headers and durations in English makes the seam more visible, not less.',
        'Duration and period strings built from a numeral obey the same partitive rule as any other numeral phrase: „30 de minute”, „90 de zile”, „24 de luni”, but „10 minute”, „3 luni”, „1 an”. Whenever a duration is composed programmatically, build the „de” into the string for values whose last two digits are 00 or 20–99.',
        'Footprint stats describe where the company IS BASED. Render „home countries” as „de țări în care avem sedii” (or „de țări în care operăm”) — never „de țări-gazdă”, which in Romanian means HOST country and reverses the relationship (it reads as 20+ countries that host us), and never „de țări de origine”, which means countries of provenance. Keep the mandatory partitive „de” because a numeral above 19 is injected in front of the label. This supersedes any earlier instruction that permitted „țări-gazdă” or „țări de origine”.',
        'Use the articulated verbal noun, not the bare infinitive/3rd-person form, whenever English has a process noun in a headline or label: „Emissions Diagnostics” = „diagnosticarea emisiilor”, not „diagnostică emisii” or „diagnostica emisiilor”; likewise „echilibrarea”, „rectificarea”, „monitorizarea”, „programarea”. Romanian forms of the type „diagnostică/echilibrează + bare plural” are finite verbs and cannot head a noun phrase. After drafting any headline, check that the first word is a noun if English had a noun.',
        'Render every date on the site in the single format DD.MM.YYYY (14.09.2026), including blog/news/press card dates, and never mix a numeric date with a long or abbreviated month form inside the same component. If an abbreviated month is ever unavoidable, Romanian uses „ian., feb., mar., apr., mai, iun., iul., aug., sept., oct., nov., dec.” — „sep.” is an English/ISO token and does not exist in Romanian.',
        'Hiring and personnel announcements: „X Welcomes Y as <title>” = „X îi urează bun venit lui/doamnei Y în funcția de …”. Never „îl/o primește pe Y”, which means receives/admits someone into a building and is off-register for a corporate announcement. This supersedes the earlier guidance that listed „îl primește pe” among the acceptable announcement verbs; keep „anunță numirea”, „îl promovează pe”, „îl angajează pe”, „îl numește pe” for their own frames.',
        'Keep each verb in an English coordinated verb list distinct in Romanian instead of collapsing two of them into one word: „collect, mine and measure data” = „a colecta, exploata și măsura date” (or „a extrage”), never „a colecta, analiza și măsura”, which both softens the extraction claim and duplicates the „Analize/analiza datelor” heading it sits under. Before delivering, check that no two items in a translated verb or noun series have become synonyms.',
        '„Input” from a tool or system is „informațiile furnizate de …”, not „datele”. Vary the referring noun when the previous sentence already used „datele echipamentelor” — repeating the same noun in adjacent sentences where English used two different words reads as flattening, not as consistency. (Terminology locks apply to product and sector terms, not to ordinary referring nouns.)',
        'When an English label uses „featuring <Brand>” for equipment built by or branded with that brand, write „marca <Brand>” or „cu tehnologie <Brand>” after the Romanian head noun — „Standuri de testare marca Bauer”. A bare „cu <Brand>” reads as „with Brand [attached/present]” and is ambiguous.',
        'Keep one preposition pattern across the whole set of parallel form/CTA labels a buyer sees in one list: all sales-inquiry labels take „pentru” — „Solicitare privind vânzările pentru sectorul aerospațial și industrial”, „… pentru transport și auto”, „… pentru asistență și consultanță”. Never alternate „vânzările pentru X” with „vânzările de X” inside the same set.',
        '„Director general” means CEO / Managing Director in Romanian — it is NOT a modifier meaning „chief”. Render C-level titles as: Chief Technology Officer = „director de tehnologie (CTO)”, Chief Financial Officer = „director financiar (CFO)”, Chief Operating Officer = „director de operațiuni (COO)”, Chief Executive Officer / General Manager / Managing Director = „director general”. Never build a title like „director general de tehnologie”, which reads as a CEO-CTO chimera and misstates a named person\'s rank. This supersedes any earlier instruction to keep „director general” inside a non-CEO C-level title.',
        'Footprint stats describe where the company OPERATES, not where it came from. „home countries” = „de țări-gazdă” (or „de țări în care operăm”), never „de țări de origine”, which means countries of provenance and makes the claim meaningless. Keep the mandatory partitive „de” because a numeral above 19 is injected in front of the label.',
        'When one English adjective modifies a whole coordinated series sharing a single head noun („embedded inspection, diagnostics, and testing processes”), attach the Romanian adjective to the HEAD noun and let the coordinated list follow with „de”: „procese încorporate de inspecție, diagnosticare și testare”. Placing the adjective after the first item only („procese de inspecție încorporate, diagnosticare și testare”) silently strips the qualifier from every other item.',
        'Repeat „mai” before every adjective in a coordinated superlative: „cele mai dificile și mai complexe provocări”, never „cele mai dificile și complexe provocări”. And keep the English possessive on the same phrase: „your toughest, most complex challenges” = „cele mai dificile și mai complexe provocări ale dumneavoastră”.',
        'Open a non-locative „Where others…” clause with the correlative „Acolo unde”, not a bare „Unde”: „Acolo unde alții văd limite, …”. A bare „Unde” is an English-shaped opener and only belongs to real physical places.',
        'aria-label, alt, title and placeholder strings are user-facing copy and must be fully translated AND semantically checked — never leave one in English and never invert a negation. „Privacy opt-out controls” names controls for REFUSING the sale/sharing of data: „Opțiuni de renunțare la vânzarea și partajarea datelor”. „Renunțare la confidențialitate” says the user gives up their privacy — the opposite of the control\'s purpose. Check every privacy, consent and cookie label for this inversion before delivering.',
        'Name the UI element by its FUNCTION in Romanian instead of calquing the English label word for word: „Chatbot input” = „Câmp de introducere a mesajului” (never „Intrare Chatbot” — intrare means entrance); „Mobile primary” = „Navigație principală mobilă”; „Download Chat” = „Descărcați conversația”; „Close Chatbot” = „Închideți chatbotul” (an English loanword used as a direct object still takes the Romanian enclitic article).',
        'Paired navigation controls must match in definiteness and length: „Anterior” / „Următor”, never „Anterior” / „Următorul”. Apply the same to Back/Next, Open/Close, Show/Hide and First/Last pairs — the visitor sees both labels side by side.',
        'Never put a comma between a noun phrase and its own participial or prepositional modifier: „Analiza stării echipamentelor bazată pe IA”, not „Analiza stării echipamentelor, bazată pe IA”. The comma detaches the modifier and makes it float. This corrects the earlier menu-label example that carried the comma.',
        'When an untranslated brand name is followed by a translated descriptor and the two would read as one run-on noun phrase, insert the same separator the sibling labels use: „Ascential Care - Servicii de asistență”, matching „Ascential Care - Acorduri de nivel de service disponibile”. Romanian cannot juxtapose a proper noun and a common-noun phrase the way English can.',
        '„Topic” in a chat, help-desk or navigation context is „subiect”, never „temă” (a school assignment or an artistic theme): „← Alegeți alt subiect”.',
        'A named plant or site inside a job title is „unitatea din X” or „fabrica din X”, never „locația X”, which is an English calque. Keep „locații” only for the plural count of global sites („40+ de locații la nivel global”).',
        'Nav, menu and footer labels stay indefinite and headline-like — no enclitic definite articles: „Istorie și mărci”, not „Istoricul și mărcile”; „Informații despre companie”, not „Informațiile despre companie”. Definite articles turn a label into a sentence fragment. Body prose keeps its articles as normal.',
        'Do not add intensifiers or adjectives the English does not contain („un scop”, not „un scop clar”), and do not flatten a motive-power metaphor into passive support: (for „powered by expertise” see the dedicated rule). The „never alimentat de” guidance applies only to software being powered by a platform.',
        'One term per asset, per FORM, with no exceptions: inside any support, service or ticketing flow the customer\'s asset is „utilaj” (or „echipament”) in EVERY field — priority definitions („utilajul sau sistemul este oprit”), location questions („unde se află utilajul”), designation fields („denumirea utilajului”) and request types („punere în funcțiune a utilajelor”). „Mașină” survives only inside product names built with the supine („Mașină de echilibrat rotoare”). This overrides the earlier „denumirea mașinii” example, which left one field out of step with the rest of its own form.',
        'Climate control / HVAC is „climatizare”, never „climă”. „Climă” means the meteorological climate of a region, so a nav column selling HVAC fan and blower balancing or vapour-compression cooling must read „Climatizare și energie”, not „Climă și energie”. Only use „climă” when the source really means weather or the environment.',
        'Physical damage to equipment is „deteriorare/deteriorări”, never „daune” — „daune” is the legal/insurance sense (compensation). Wear and damage on a machine: „uzură sau deteriorări minore”, „deteriorări ale rulmenților”.',
        'When an English adjective distributes over a coordinated pair („minor wear/damage”, „faulty sensors and cables”), place the Romanian adjective after the last noun in the plural so it governs both — „uzură sau deteriorări minore”, not „uzură minoră/deteriorări”, which silently leaves the second noun unqualified and changes the severity.',
        'Press-release announcement frame: „X Announces Y as <title>” is „X anunță numirea lui Y / a doamnei Y în funcția de …”. Romanian announces an appointment, not a person into a role, so never write „îl/o anunță pe Y în funcția de …”. Keep the existing idiomatic verbs for the other frames (îl promovează pe, îl angajează pe, îl numește pe, îi urează bun venit lui Y).',
        'Put a scope adjective (global, regional, european, mondial) directly on the head noun it modifies, not at the end of a genitive chain where it attaches to the wrong noun: „operațiunilor globale de fabricație”, never „operațiunilor de fabricație globale”; „vânzărilor globale pentru sectorul …”.',
        'Keep C-level rank in the Romanian job title. Chief Technology Officer = „director general de tehnologie” (or „CTO”), Chief Financial Officer = „director general financiar”. A bare „director de tehnologie” reads a rank junior beside „vicepreședinte senior” and „vicepreședinte executiv” on the same page.',
        'Aerospace & industrials is a pair of ADJECTIVES in Romanian: the nav label is „Aerospațial și industrial” and any full phrase is „sectorul aerospațial și industrial”. Never pair the adjective „aerospațial” with the noun „industrie” („aerospațial și industrie”), in nav labels, tab buttons, job titles or form options — the buyer reads them in sequence.',
        'Lock „training” to „instruire” everywhere on the site — „Instruire și consultanță”, „Cursuri de instruire”, „Seria de instruire AECD și OBD”. Do not alternate with „formare”, which on a technical-services site reads as vocational training and looks like a second translator.',
        'Lock „request” to „solicitare” in every form string — „Ce tip de solicitare este aceasta?”, „Subiectul solicitării”, „Vă rugăm să descrieți solicitarea mai detaliat.” Never mix „cerere” and „solicitare” inside one form; the fields sit within a screen of each other.',
        '„Service” (an on-site intervention) and „support” are different words: „Where is service needed?” = „Unde este necesară intervenția de service?”, not „…asistența?”. Keep „asistență” exclusively for „support”.',
        'One Romanian word per sense of „machine”: „utilaj” in support, service and shop-floor copy („Aveți un utilaj oprit din funcțiune”, „unde se află utilajul”), „echipament(e)” in product, analytics and value-prop copy („starea echipamentelor”, „datele echipamentelor”). Keep „mașină” only inside machine names built with the supine („Mașină de echilibrat rotoare”). Never use „mașină” for a generic machine on a site that also sells automotive equipment.',
        'Align short mega-menu labels with the body copy for the same product: if the body says „analize privind starea echipamentelor”, the menu label for „AI-driven machine analytics” is „Analiza stării echipamentelor, bazată pe IA”, not a fresh paraphrase like „Analiza datelor de la mașini”. A label a buyer reads next to the paragraph must use the paragraph\'s term.',
        '„Facility” in a service form is a plant or site: „unitate”, „fabrică”, „sediu” — „În cadrul unității dumneavoastră”. „Instalație” is a false friend meaning an installation/system of equipment (instalație electrică), so „în cadrul instalației dumneavoastră” asks the buyer where the machine is inside their wiring.',
        'Do not coordinate the invariable adjective „auto” with a „de + noun” genitive under one head noun. Expand both sides into parallel prepositional phrases: „inițiativele din domeniul autovehiculelor și al camioanelor”, not „inițiativele auto și de camioane”.',
        'Do not repeat the same auxiliary or verb across adjacent short UI sentences just because Romanian grammar allows it — English punch is lost. „Need support now? Machine down or need a hand?” = „Aveți nevoie de asistență acum? Utilaj oprit din funcțiune sau nevoie de o mână de ajutor?”: drop the second and third „aveți” and let the ellipsis carry it.',
        'Data-protection wording is fixed by Romanian GDPR usage: „date/informații cu caracter personal”, including when an adjective is added — „informațiile mele sensibile cu caracter personal”. Never alternate with „informații personale” in adjacent links.',
        'Form the English „non-” prefix with the Romanian prefix „ne-”, written as one word: „neurgentă”, „nedistructiv”, „neplanificat”. „non-urgentă” is a calque.',
        'Never add a universal quantifier the English does not have. „across your production equipment” = „pentru echipamentele dumneavoastră de producție” — keep the plural and „dumneavoastră” (breadth), but do not write „toate/fiecare/întregul”, which turns breadth into a completeness promise the company has not made.',
        '„Actionable” information/insight must keep the act-on-it claim: „informații pe baza cărora se poate acționa”. „Informații utile” is a downgrade of the core analytics claim, and „acționabil” remains banned (it means legally actionable in Romanian).',
        'Capitalise the first character of every standalone nav, menu and footer label, even when Romanian word order moves a common noun to the front of a brand name: „Divizia Ascential Medical & Life Sciences”, not „divizia Ascential …”. The label is a heading, not a sentence fragment.',
        'Localise every sticky-section-nav and tab pill label, including single English words that look like loanwords: „Hub” = „Resurse” (the page already uses „Centrul de resurse”), „Featured” = „Evidențiat”. One English pill beside seven Romanian ones is the most visible seam on the page.',
        'Counter, stat and badge labels are rendered with a JS-injected numeral immediately before them (70+, 20+, 2300+, 40+), so the label string itself must start with the mandatory partitive \'de\': „de ani de inovație”, „de țări de origine”, „de specialiști”, „de locații la nivel global”. Whenever a short label will be prefixed by a number above 19, build the \'de\' into the label — never translate the label as if it stood alone.',
        'Never render an English industry modifier as a bare noun attached to another noun ("metals automation", "equipment data", "diesel turbocharger balancing"). Rebuild it as a prepositional or genitive phrase naming the sector or owner: „automatizare și integrare de sisteme pentru industria metalelor”, „datele echipamentelor”, „echilibrarea turbosuflantelor diesel” — never „automatizarea metalelor” (which means automating the metal itself) or „date de echipament”.',
        'A brand or product name used attributively in English goes AFTER the Romanian head noun, never before it: "Burke Porter ADAS systems" = „Sisteme ADAS Burke Porter”, not „Burke Porter sisteme ADAS”. Apply this even in short mega-menu labels where the English order would fit.',
        'Romanian does not use the serial (Oxford) comma before the final „și” in a list, and every item of a coordinated list governed by one head noun must carry the SAME case and part of speech. „din sectoarele transporturilor, medical și științe ale vieții, și industrial” is broken three ways; write „din sectoarele transporturilor, medicinei și științelor vieții, precum și din industrie”. When a list mixes nouns and adjectives in English, convert them all to genitive nouns or split the last item off with „precum și din …”.',
        'Adjectives that name a sector (aerospațial, industrial, medical, auto) may stand alone only in a clipped nav label. Inside a full phrase, give them a head noun: „Soluții pentru sectorul aerospațial și industrial”, „Solicitare privind vânzările pentru sectorul aerospațial și industrial” — never „pentru aerospațial și industrie”, which yokes an adjective to a noun.',
        'Use „etalonare” only for metrological instrument calibration and certification. For ADAS and other automotive-aftermarket service copy, the Romanian trade term is „calibrare”: „Sisteme de calibrare ADAS pentru piața secundară”, „calibrare ADAS”. Keep the form-label „Etalonare / Certificare” as is.',
        'Do not use „analitică” or „analitice” as a standalone noun — it is an anglicism. Write „analiza datelor”, „echipele noastre de asistență și de analiză a datelor”, „datele analitice privind …”.',
        '„Machine down” in support copy means a breakdown, not a switched-off machine: write „Aveți un utilaj oprit din funcțiune” or „utilaj defect”, never „Mașina este oprită”. Prefer „utilaj/echipament” over „mașină” on any site that also sells automotive equipment, because „mașină” reads first as „car”.',
        'Translate English business calques into real Romanian idiom rather than word-for-word: mission-critical = „de importanță critică” (not „critice pentru misiune”); a strategic move = „un demers strategic” (not „o mișcare strategică”); from a blank slate = „de la zero” (not „de la o pagină albă”); contract balancing as-a-service = „Servicii de echilibrare în regim contractual” (not „echilibrare în contract”); gauging = „control dimensional” when it means production metrology.',
        '„Comenzi” means purchase orders in Romanian business copy. For machine "controls" write „sisteme de comandă” or „comenzi ale mașinii” with an explicit qualifier — never a bare „comenzi” on a site full of order and request forms.',
        'Keep coordinated nominalisations parallel: if the first item is a definite nominalisation, the second must match — „rectificarea și texturarea cilindrilor”, never „rectificarea cilindrilor, texturare”.',
        'Pick ONE Romanian rendering per repeated concept and use it in the page title, the nav, the body and the footer alike: assembly = „asamblare” (never „montaj”, including in the SEO <title>); corporate information = „Informații despre companie” in both nav and footer; marketplace = „Magazin de piese de schimb” with the same article in nav and footer; machine health = „starea echipamentelor”; unplanned downtime = „timpii de nefuncționare neplanificați” (keep it plural everywhere); press = „comunicate de presă”, not „știri”.',
        'Do not double a possessive that the clitic already expresses: „Ne ghidează un scop clar și ne susține expertiza”, not „… expertiza noastră”. Likewise drop the subject pronoun „noi” when the verb ending already carries it, especially if „noi” also appears as the adjective „new” nearby.',
        'Prefer the natural Romanian clitic-first order and concise phrasing in CTAs and form helper text: „Ne puteți suna …” rather than „Puteți să ne sunați …”; „Vă rugăm să descrieți cererea mai detaliat.” rather than „… cererea dumneavoastră cu mai multe detalii.”',
        '„Scheduling of service” is an on-site intervention, not an abstract service: write „programarea unei intervenții de service”, never „programarea serviciului”.',
        'Romanian forms the gerund plus an enclitic pronoun with the linking vowel -u-: permițându-ne, oferindu-vă, ajutându-i, adresându-se. Forms like „permițând-ne" or „oferind-vă" do not exist in Romanian — always insert the -u- before the clitic.',
        'A predicative adjective or participle after a fi must agree in gender and number with its subject, including in short UI banners and questions: „Mașina este oprită", „Sistemul este oprit", „Liniile sunt oprite". Before delivering any string containing „este/sunt + participiu", check the participle against the real gender of the noun — mașină, linie, instalație, platformă are feminine.',
        'Lock one Romanian term per source concept and reuse it in every string of the site, however short: assembly = asamblare (never montaj); aerospace & industrials = aerospațial și industrie (never aeronautică, never aeronavală); transportation = transport (never transporturi); automotive = auto (never automotive); calibration = etalonare; NDT = control nedistructiv (CND); aftermarket = piața secundară; end-of-line = capăt de linie (never final de linie); powertrain = grup motopropulsor; resource hub = centrul de resurse (never hub-ul); press releases = comunicate de presă (never comunicări de presă); marketplace = magazin (never piață); refrigerant = agent frigorific; throughput = debit de producție. Nav labels, form labels and body prose must all use the same choice, because the buyer reads them in sequence.',
        'Never render an English "inquiry" as interogație or interogare — in Romanian those mean a police interrogation or a database query. A commercial enquiry is „solicitare" (or „cerere de ofertă"): „Solicitare privind vânzările pentru transport și auto". Keep the source preposition: never collapse it into a headless noun stack („Interogare vânzări transporturi").',
        'Short navigation and product labels may not use bare noun + noun modification, even though it fits in the same number of characters as the English. Use a definite genitive or a preposition, and repeat the genitival article across coordinated items: „automatizarea metalelor și integrarea sistemelor" (not „automatizare metale și integrare sisteme"), „echilibrarea turbinelor și a motoarelor cu reacție", „detecția scurgerilor", „rectificarea cilindrilor", „construcția rețelelor de utilități", „Testarea sistemelor de acționare și a trenului de aterizare", „Cerințe privind lanțul de aprovizionare".',
        'Keep an epistemic verb epistemic: predict = „anticipați" or „preziceți", detect = „detectați", monitor = „monitorizați". Only the English "prevent" may become „preveniți". Rendering "Predict failures before they happen" as „Preveniți defecțiunile" promises that the product stops the failure, which is a claim the company does not make.',
        'Preserve plural scope and possessives in benefit claims. An English plural covering a fleet stays plural in Romanian and keeps "your": "across your production equipment" = „pentru toate echipamentele dumneavoastră de producție", never „pe întregul echipament de producție", which reads as one single machine and drops the customer\'s ownership.',
        'Corporate-structure words are not interchangeable: affiliate = „companie afiliată" / „afiliate", division = „divizie", subsidiary = „filială", and „partener" is reserved for third parties outside the group. Translating a footer heading "Affiliates:" as „Parteneri:" restates the company\'s ownership structure and collides with the separate „Parteneri" support-partner menu.',
        'Job titles and region names are translated and written lowercase inside a Romanian sentence: „în funcția de vicepreședinte senior al operațiunilor de fabricație globale", „în funcția de director regional pentru aerospațial și industrie", „pentru regiunea Americilor". Never leave "Senior Vice President of Global Manufacturing Operations", "Regional Director" or "Americas" standing in English, and never capitalise the title mid-sentence as English does. A bare plural region name needs an article or preposition: „Americile", „regiunea Americilor", never „Americi".',
        'Rewrite dangling English participles as finite Romanian clauses. "and counting" is not „și încă numărând" (meaningless in Romanian) but „și numărul continuă să crească". "causing production interruption" becomes a coordinated clause: „și producția se întrerupe". "driven by purpose" is not „conduși de scop" but „Ne ghidează un scop clar".',
        'In a defining relative clause about a place or a situation, use „în care", not the locative „unde": „Probleme critice, în care mașina sau sistemul este oprit". „unde" belongs to real physical locations.',
        'When the source attaches one head noun to a coordinated list, repeat the preposition so the head noun reaches every item: "Chassis, hub & steerable dyno" = „Dinamometre de șasiu, de butuc și pentru roți direcționale". Attaching the head noun only to the last item silently removes the first items from the category.',
        'Use the Romanian institutional or shop-floor term for form and UI labels, not the cognate: "State / Province" = „Județ / Provincie / Stat" (a bare „Stat" is a sovereign state); "Asset serial number" = „Numărul de serie al echipamentului" (an „activ" is an accounting asset); "machine designation" = „denumirea mașinii"; "Skip to content" = „Salt la conținut"; "global locations" = „locații la nivel global"; "expert people" = „specialiști"; "unlock performance" = „valorificați potențialul" (a debloca is for doors and phones).',
        '"Watch" in front of a video is „Urmăriți", not „Vedeți"; keep „Vedeți" for "See".',
        'Service and support copy stays in the same professional register as the rest of the site: „echipa noastră se va ocupa imediat de solicitarea dumneavoastră", never the colloquial „se va apuca imediat de lucru". A capital-equipment vendor\'s support banner is not conversational.',
        'Several strings on one page are variants of the same sentence — a visible heading and its sticky-nav label, a nav flyout card and the news-grid card for the same press release, a plain-text label and the same label wrapped in HTML. Translate all variants identically, word for word, because the visitor sees them within one screen of each other; shipping „aducem totul împreună" in the heading and „aducem totul laolaltă" in the nav is read as machine output.',
        'Keep only the brand name inside <span class="tmm-brand-word">…</span> and leave the descriptor outside it, exactly as in the English source: <span class="tmm-brand-word">Lismar</span> inspecție CND. Pulling the Romanian descriptor into the brand span makes plain text render with brand styling.',
        '"Off-highway & mobile" is "Utilaje off-highway și echipamente mobile". "mobilă" alone means furniture.',
        'Direct objects that are people or named entities take "pe" plus the resumptive clitic: "îl numește pe X", "o promovează pe Y".',
        'Never splice an English gerund into a Romanian verb ("s-a rebranding"). Write "și-a schimbat numele în" or "a fost redenumită".',
        'Keep brand names whole and identical everywhere: never translate part of "XL Machine" or any other brand.',
        'NEVER let two coordinated verbs share one complement when they govern different cases. This exact sentence has failed four review rounds: \'We deliver and support intelligent systems\' rendered as \'Furnizăm și asigurăm mentenanța sistemelor inteligente\' is broken, because \'furnizăm\' needs an ACCUSATIVE object (\'furnizăm sisteme\') while \'asigurăm mentenanța\' forces a GENITIVE (\'a sistemelor\'), so the delivery claim collapses. Write \'Furnizăm și întreținem sisteme inteligente\' with one shared accusative, or split into two clauses: \'Furnizăm sisteme inteligente și le asigurăm mentenanța\'. Before delivering, check every coordinated verb pair for this.',
        'Agree every object clitic with its noun\'s real gender and number — feminine plurals take \'le\', never the default \'îi\'; check the clitic against the noun before shipping.',
        'Never let coordinated verbs share a single complement when they govern different cases; repeat the object in each verb\'s own case or resume it with a clitic.',
        'Set an appositive brand or product name off with commas immediately after the noun it names; never strand it at the end of that noun\'s modifiers.',
        'Translate process and defect vocabulary with the established shop-floor term (rework = \'reprelucrare\'), not a general-purpose paraphrase.',
        'Agree object clitics with the noun\'s real gender: use \'le\' for feminine plurals, \'îi\' only for masculine; never default to \'îi\'.',
        'Use \'pe\' plus a resumptive clitic only for definite, specific human objects; drop both before bare indefinite plurals like \'echipe\'.',
        'Never share one complement between coordinated verbs with different case government; repeat the object in its own case or resume it with a clitic.',
        'Keep every item in a list grammatically parallel — all adjectives, all bare nouns, or all genitives — and repeat the genitival article across the series.',
        'Attach a brand name directly to the noun it names, set off by commas, instead of stranding it after modifiers.',
        'Invert to verb-then-subject when the subject carries a long relative clause; do not front-load heavy subjects as English does.',
        'Keep the supine for machine names (\'mașini de echilibrat\') and keep rendering \'consistent\' as \'uniform/constant\', never \'consistent\'.',
        'Mark every definite or individuated human direct object with \'pe\' plus a clitic that AGREES in gender and number with it — îl/o/îi/le (\'le ajută pe echipe\', \'îi reunește pe cei mai buni tehnicieni\', \'pentru a-i sprijini pe clienți\') — in every clause, including superlatives, coordinated verbs and infinitives; never default to \'îi\', and never use \'pe\' in one sentence then drop it in the next.',
        'Respect case government: give each coordinated verb its own complement in the case it requires instead of sharing one noun phrase between an accusative verb and a genitive nominalisation — write \'Furnizăm sisteme inteligente și le asigurăm mentenanța\', never \'Livrăm și asigurăm mentenanța sistemelor\'.',
        'Keep a genitive enumeration in the genitive all the way to the last item: repeat a/al/ai/ale before each element and close with \'și a altor aspecte\' or \'și a multora\', never with a nominative tail such as \'și multe altele\'.',
        'Keep every enumeration grammatically parallel — all items the same part of speech, case and article pattern; never mix an adjective, a bare noun and a definite noun phrase in one industry or feature list (\'auto, electric, pompe … și industria feroviară\').',
        'Fix one gender, plural, spelling and Romanian equivalent per technical term and reuse it across the whole file — rotor is neuter (rotoare, rotoarelor), never mixed with rotori/rotorilor — and keep the dumneavoastră register, comma-below diacritics ș/ț (never ş/ţ) and the mandatory \'de\' after numerals above 19 everywhere.',
        'Reject Latinate look-alikes and English calques and say it the Romanian way: consistent means thick, so use uniform/constant/reproductibil; acționabil is legally actionable, use util; împuternicit is legally authorized, use \'au libertatea/puterea să\'; \'a se conecta cu\' is technical, use \'a lua legătura cu\'; avansare means promotion, use \'progresul tehnologic\'; \'working toward a degree\' is \'urmează un master la…\'.',
        'Read every phrase as Romanian, not as English: verify each verb-noun and adjective-noun pair actually collocates and change the NOUN rather than the adjective when it does not (\'soluții rapide\', never \'posibilități rapide\'), and use Romanian information order — invert to verb-subject instead of fronting a long indefinite subject before a short predicate (\'Ne acordă încredere producătorii care…\').',
        'Punctuate and spell to Romanian standard: comma-below ș/ț (never cedilla ş/ţ), â inside words and î at word edges, decimal comma, mandatory \'de\' after numerals above 19, and quotations opening with „ and closing with ” at both ends.',
        'Respect case government: give each coordinated verb a complement in the case it requires instead of sharing one noun phrase between an accusative verb and a genitive nominalisation (write \'furnizăm sisteme și le asigurăm mentenanța\', not \'livrăm și asigurăm mentenanța sistemelor\'), and in a genitive series repeat a/al/ai/ale before every item, keeping the final item in the same case (\'și a altor aspecte\', never \'și altele\').',
        'Keep every enumeration grammatically parallel: all items the same part of speech, case and article pattern — never mix an adjective, a bare noun and a full noun phrase in one industry or feature list.',
        'Reformulate English and French corporate abstractions instead of transcoding them word for word (working toward a degree → urmează un master la…, deep expertise → vasta noastră experiență, responsiveness → promptitudine, global customers → clienți din întreaga lume, plant systems → sistemele uzinei), and re-read each adjective-noun pair to confirm it actually collocates in Romanian.',
        'Punctuate to Romanian standard: comma-below ș/ț (never cedilla ş/ţ), â inside words and î at word edges, decimal comma, mandatory \'de\' after numerals above 19 — and check BOTH ends of every quotation: it must open with „ and close with ”, never a straight " or English “ ”.',
        'Mark definite human direct objects with \'pe\' plus the matching clitic in every clause, including infinitives and superlatives (\'îi reunește pe cei mai buni tehnicieni\', \'pentru a-i sprijini pe clienți\'); never drop it in one sentence after using it correctly in another.',
        'Use the term the Romanian specialist uses, not the nearest cognate or a neighbouring part: journal = fus (lagăr = bearing), roll = cilindru, probe = palpator, NDT = CND, eddy current = curenți turbionari; name machines with the supine (mașină de echilibrat, de rectificat).',
        'Reject Latinate look-alikes that mean something else in Romanian — acționabil (legally actionable), împuternicit (legally authorized), consistent (thick), eventual (final), a realiza (to understand) — and express the idea with a plain Romanian construction instead.',
        'Rewrite English marketing abstractions as concrete Romanian statements rather than matching them noun for noun: excel in performance, engineer new possibilities, empowered, deep expertise, actionable insights, machine health, focused on advancing technology all require a full reformulation.',
        'Keep terminology mechanically consistent across the whole file: one plural and one spelling per technical noun (rotoare/rotoarelor, never mixed with rotori/rotorilor), one Romanian equivalent per repeated source term, and the dumneavoastră register everywhere.',
        'Keep every enumeration grammatically parallel — same part of speech, same case, same article pattern for all items; in a genitive series repeat the possessive article (\'asupra opririlor, a stării, a reparațiilor\') and close it with \'și altele\', not a nominative phrase.',
        'After drafting, re-read the Romanian alone and fix what only makes sense in English: clitics (o, îl, le) must agree with a real Romanian antecedent, modifiers must not repeat their noun (industria industrială), and English noun stacks must become prepositional phrases — aplicații din domeniul transporturilor, sistemele uzinei.',
        'Use comma-below ș/ț (never cedilla ş/ţ), â inside words and î at word edges, Romanian quotation marks „…” (never straight " or English “”), decimal comma, period as thousands separator, and the mandatory \'de\' after numerals above 19 (500 de companii).',
        'Name machines with the Romanian supine pattern \'mașină de + verb\' — mașină de echilibrat, de găurit, de rectificat, de încercat — never \'mașină de + substantiv verbal\' (mașină de echilibrare).',
        'Translate established technical terminology into the Romanian standard used in the field (curenți turbionari, control nedistructiv/CND, fus vs lagăr, palpator, cilindru de laminor); never leave one English method name sitting beside translated sibling terms in the same list.',
        'Check every English–Romanian look-alike before reusing it: consistent→uniform/constant, actual→curent, eventual→posibil, sensibil→semnificativ, a realiza→a înțelege/a obține. Pick the true Romanian equivalent, not the cognate.',
        'Respect Romanian case and clitic rules: \'a se alătura\' takes a dative noun, so insert a common noun before a brand (\'se alătură companiei X\'); definite animate direct objects need \'pe\' plus a resumptive clitic (\'a-i sprijini pe clienți\').',
        'Rewrite English business and marketing metaphors into idiomatic Romanian instead of translating them word for word — sharp minds, deep expertise, actionable insights, excel in performance, machine health, downtime, empowered, engineer possibilities all need a Romanian reformulation.',
        'Keep coordinated lists grammatically parallel: every item the same part of speech, same case and same article pattern; never mix an untranslated English adjective with Romanian nouns in one enumeration.',
        'Replace English words that have settled Romanian equivalents (website→site web, accessibility→accesibilitatea site-ului, Program Owner→responsabil de program, automotive→auto, business analytics→analiza datelor de afaceri); keep English only for product, platform and brand names.',
        'Romanian uses ș and ț with COMMA below (U+0219, U+021B), not cedilla (ş, ţ). Always emit the comma-below forms. This is the single most common tell of a machine translation.',
        'Use â inside words and î at the start/end of words: "român", "începe", "a coborî".',
        'Capitalize only the first word of headlines and sentences. Never carry over English title case.',
        'Months and days are lowercase mid-sentence: "luni", "ianuarie".',
        'Decimal separator is comma: 1,5 milioane. Thousands separator is period: 10.000.',
        'Date format is DD.MM.YYYY (14.04.2026).',
        'Use 24-hour time (14:30).',
        'Currency follows the number: "1.500 RON" or "1.500 lei".',
        'The definite article is a SUFFIX, not a separate word: "soluția", not "the soluție". Watch for accidental double articles when translating "the solution".',
        'Nouns have gender and the adjective must agree: "soluție completă" (f), "sistem complet" (m). Re-check every adjective after translating.',
        'Translate "Learn more" as "Aflați mai multe", never "Învățați mai multe" (which means "study more").',
        'Translate "Get started" as "Începeți" or "Începeți acum", never a literal "Obțineți început".',
        'Translate "Sign up" as "Creați un cont" or "Înregistrați-vă", not "Semnați".',
        'Translate "Free trial" as "Perioadă de probă gratuită", not "Proces liber".',
        'Translate "data-driven" as "bazat pe date", not "condus de date".',
        'Translate "best-in-class" as "cel mai bun din categorie" or "de top".',
        'Translate "scalable" as "scalabil" — check gender agreement with its noun.',
        'Translate "stakeholder" as "parte interesată"; keep "stakeholder" only in finance-heavy copy.',
        'Translate "ROI" as "ROI" or "randamentul investiției" — pick one and stay consistent.',
        'Translate "case study" as "studiu de caz"; plural "studii de caz".',
        'Translate "Powered by" as "Susținut de" or "Funcționează cu", never "Alimentat de" for software.',
        'Translate "Trusted by 500+ companies" as "Peste 500 de companii au încredere în noi" — note the obligatory "de" after numbers above 19.',
        'Numbers above 19 take "de" before the noun: "500 de companii", not "500 companii". This is mandatory and its absence reads as foreign.',
        'For industrial/engineering copy prefer established technical terms: "echilibrare" (balancing), "standuri de test" (test stands), "traductor" (transducer), "etalonare" (calibration).',
        'Translate "measurement systems" as "sisteme de măsurare", not "sisteme de măsurătoare" in a product context.',
        'Use "mentenanță" for industrial maintenance; "întreținere" is more domestic/general.',
    ),
    'avoid' => array(
        'Cedilla forms ş/ţ instead of comma-below ș/ț — the clearest machine-translation tell.',
        'Dropping "de" after numerals above 19 ("500 companii" instead of "500 de companii").',
        'English title case in headlines.',
        'Addressing a business reader as "tu".',
        'Literal "Învățați mai multe" for "Learn more".',
        'Calques such as "face sens" for "make sense" — use "are sens".',
        'Overusing "a realiza" for "realize" when the meaning is "to notice" — that is "a-și da seama".',
        'Leaving English gerunds untranslated ("Monitoring", "Testing") when a Romanian noun exists.',
    ),
    'examples' => array(
        array( 'en' => 'Get started today.',                  'translation' => 'Începeți chiar astăzi.' ),
        array( 'en' => 'Trusted by 500+ companies.',          'translation' => 'Peste 500 de companii au încredere în noi.' ),
        array( 'en' => 'Book a demo.',                        'translation' => 'Programați o demonstrație.' ),
        array( 'en' => 'Contact us',                          'translation' => 'Contactați-ne' ),
        array( 'en' => 'Read more',                           'translation' => 'Citiți mai mult' ),
        array( 'en' => 'Test and measurement systems',        'translation' => 'Sisteme de testare și măsurare' ),
        array( 'en' => 'Service and support',                 'translation' => 'Service și asistență' ),
        array( 'en' => 'Spare parts',                         'translation' => 'Piese de schimb' ),
        array( 'en' => 'Precision balancing',                 'translation' => 'Echilibrare de precizie' ),
    ),
);
